      "id": 2,
      "claim": "The obstetric conjugate (shortest anteroposterior diameter) of the typical adult female pelvic inlet is approximately 110 mm (11 cm).",
      "evidence": "Obstetric anatomy textbooks (e.g., Cunningham et al., 2014) list the average obstetric conjugate at ~110 mm.",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "The adult human skull is a rigid structure with fused sutures that cannot compress or deform significantly to reduce its diameter.",
      "evidence": "Biomechanical properties of bone (high Young's modulus) and the fusion of cranial sutures in adults prevent the molding possible in neonates.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "The smallest dimension of the adult skull (140 mm) is significantly larger than the shortest dimension of the pelvic inlet (110 mm).",
      "evidence": "Comparison of 140 mm (skull width) vs 110 mm (pelvic obstetric conjugate) shows a 30 mm deficit.",
      "weight": 0.2,
      "depends_on": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Therefore, an adult human skull cannot physically pass through the birth canal without catastrophic fracture of the skull or pelvis.",
      "evidence": "Geometric constraint: a rigid object with a minimum dimension larger than the aperture's minimum dimension cannot pass through.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [3, 4]
    }
  ],
  "answer": "No",
